From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: JR Lomas <lomas.jr@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 14:07:40 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] stm32: Batch pending bxCAN receive frames

bxCAN FIFO 0 can hold three complete frames, but the interrupt handler consumed only one entry per invocation.  When a burst filled multiple slots, the still-asserted receive source forced the Cortex-M through another exception return and entry for each remaining frame and repeated transmit and error-status checks which were unrelated to that receive batch.

Interpret FMP0 as the entry-time FIFO occupancy and consume that many records before continuing through the rest of the handler.  This amortizes interrupt prologue, epilogue, and shared-vector work across up to three frames while preserving FIFO order and returning scarce receive slots earlier.

Deliberately snapshot the occupancy instead of polling the live FIFO until it becomes empty.  A busy bus may refill capacity while the handler runs; following that moving target would make interrupt residency unbounded and could delay scheduler and timer work.  Later arrivals leave the level-sensitive source asserted for a subsequent bounded invocation.

No filter, identifier, payload, acknowledgement, or client-visible wire behavior changes.  The affected STM32F072 bxCAN configuration compiles and links with the bounded batch path.

Signed-off-by: JR Lomas <lomas.jr@gmail.com>

diff --git a/src/stm32/can.c b/src/stm32/can.c
index f62fe542..eb224c95 100644
--- a/src/stm32/can.c
+++ b/src/stm32/can.c
@@ -230,7 +230,25 @@ CAN_IRQHandler(void)
         SOC_CAN->RF0R = CAN_RF0R_FOVR0;
         CAN_Errors.rx_overflows++;
     }
-    if (rf0r & CAN_RF0R_FMP0) {
+    /*
+     * FMP0 is the number of complete frames already resident in the three-slot
+     * FIFO, not merely a boolean pending flag.  Processing one entry and
+     * returning leaves the interrupt asserted for the next entry, forcing the
+     * Cortex-M to repeat exception return and entry plus every unrelated status
+     * check below.  Snapshot the count and process that arrival batch in one
+     * invocation so the fixed interrupt cost is paid once for up to three CAN
+     * frames.
+     *
+     * Use the entry snapshot instead of polling FMP0 until empty.  A
+     * continuously busy bus can refill a slot after RFOM0 returns it; chasing
+     * that live level would make interrupt residency traffic-dependent and
+     * could postpone higher-level scheduler work indefinitely.  Any frame
+     * arriving after the snapshot leaves FMP0 asserted and causes the normal
+     * next interrupt, while this invocation remains strictly bounded by the
+     * hardware FIFO depth.
+     */
+    uint32_t pending = rf0r & CAN_RF0R_FMP0;
+    while (pending--) {
         // Read and ack data packet
         CAN_FIFOMailBox_TypeDef *mb = &SOC_CAN->sFIFOMailBox[0];
         uint32_t rir = mb->RIR;
